<ToolLayout
  title="Medicare Donut Hole Calculator 2026 — Free | Seniors Audit"
  description="Free Medicare donut hole calculator — track your 2026 Part D $2,000 out-of-pocket cap in seconds. Updated for Inflation Reduction Act. No account."
  faqs={faqs}
  howToSteps={howToSteps}
  datePublished="2026-07-01"
  dateModified="2026-07-29"
  agency="CMS"
  canonical="https://www.seniorsaudit.com/tools/medicare-donut-hole-calculator/"
  keywords="medicare donut hole calculator, coverage gap calculator medicare, medicare coverage gap 2026, part d out of pocket cap 2026, troop calculator medicare"
>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 2 — HERO SECTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="dh-hero">
    <div class="dh-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb navigation">
      <a href="/medicare/" class="dh-breadcrumb-link">Medicare</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> › </span>
      <a href="/tools/" class="dh-breadcrumb-link">Tools &amp; Calculators</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> › </span>
      <span>Donut Hole Calculator</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="dh-h1">Medicare Donut Hole &amp; Part D Coverage Gap Calculator 2026</h1>
    <p class="dh-date">Figures last verified against official sources in {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <!-- AEO Direct Answer Block -->
    <div class="dh-aeo-block" role="note" aria-label="Quick Answer">
      <p class="dh-aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="dh-aeo-text">
        Under the Inflation Reduction Act, the old Medicare Part D donut hole (coverage gap) is replaced in 2026 by a hard <strong class="dh-gold">$2,000 annual out-of-pocket spending cap</strong>. Once your true out-of-pocket (TrOOP) spending reaches $2,000, you pay <strong class="dh-gold">$0 for covered prescription drugs</strong> for the rest of the calendar year.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Problem Statement -->
    <p class="dh-problem">
      We noticed that many seniors dread the start of each year because high prescription costs on brand-name medications used to trigger the "donut hole" penalty gap. We built this free tool because seniors deserve a fast, clear, plain English calculator to track their progress toward the new 2026 federal $2,000 cap — completely private with zero accounts or signups required.
    </p>

    <!-- Who This Tool Is For -->
    <ul class="dh-who-list" aria-label="Who this tool is for">
      <li class="dh-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="dh-who-arrow">→</span>
        Seniors taking brand-name or high-tier medications who want to track how quickly they will reach the $2,000 out-of-pocket limit.
      </li>
      <li class="dh-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="dh-who-arrow">→</span>
        Medicare beneficiaries trying to decide whether to enroll in the Medicare Prescription Payment Plan (M3P) monthly payment program.
      </li>
      <li class="dh-who-item">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="dh-who-arrow">→</span>
        Adult children helping a parent calculate annual out-of-pocket drug costs across Medicare Part D or Medicare Advantage plans.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 3 — THE TOOL INTERFACE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="dh-tool-container" id="calculator">
    <DonutHoleTool client:load />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 5 — NO SIGNUP COMPETITIVE POSITIONING
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <NoSignupStatement />

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 4 — HOW TO USE SECTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="dh-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-use-heading">
    <h2 id="how-to-use-heading">How to Use This Tool — Step by Step</h2>
    <ol class="dh-steps-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Step 1 — Find your current out-of-pocket drug spending.</strong> Look at your latest monthly Explanation of Benefits (EOB) statement from your Medicare Part D prescription plan or Medicare Advantage plan. Find the line marked "True Out-of-Pocket" (TrOOP) year-to-date total.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 2 — Enter your year-to-date spending into the calculator.</strong> Type your current dollar total into the field above. If you are planning for a new year starting in January, enter $0.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 3 — Enter your estimated monthly prescription copays.</strong> Add up the typical copay amounts you pay each month at the pharmacy counter for all your regular medications and type that monthly average into the tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 4 — Click "Calculate My Coverage Status."</strong> The calculator calculates your exact milestone month when your copays will drop to $0 for the rest of 2026.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Pro Tip -->
    <div class="dh-pro-tip-box">
      <p class="dh-pro-tip-title">💡 Pro Tip — Spread High Upfront Costs with M3P</p>
      <p class="dh-pro-tip-text">
        If you take expensive medications that would cause you to hit the $2,000 cap early in the year, you can opt into the Medicare Prescription Payment Plan (M3P) through your plan provider. M3P splits your out-of-pocket costs into equal monthly payments across the year without interest or fees. Learn how to opt in at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/prescription-payment-plan" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov/prescription-payment-plan</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 5 — REAL-LIFE SCENARIOS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="dh-section dh-section-tinted" aria-labelledby="scenarios-heading">
    <h2 id="scenarios-heading">Real-Life Examples — How This Works in Practice</h2>

    <!-- Scenario 1 -->
    <div class="dh-scenario-card">
      <div class="dh-scenario-header">
        <span class="dh-scenario-tag">Example 1</span>
        <strong class="dh-scenario-title">Catherine, 68 — Takes Specialty Blood Thinner &amp; Diabetes Medication</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="dh-scenario-intro">Catherine takes Eliquis ($150/month copay) and Jardiance ($100/month copay), totaling $250 per month in out-of-pocket pharmacy bills.</p>
      <table class="dh-scenario-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Input</th>
            <th>Value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>YTD Out-of-Pocket Spend (January 1)</td>
            <td>$0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Monthly Copays</td>
            <td>$250 / month</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Part D Deductible (Plan standard)</td>
            <td>$590</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="dh-scenario-result">
        Catherine reaches her $2,000 cap by <strong>August</strong> (8 months × $250 = $2,000). For September, October, November, and December, her copays are <span class="dh-amber-highlight">$0 per month</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="dh-scenario-action">Catherine signed up for M3P payment smoothing, dividing her $2,000 total into steady $166.67 monthly bills all year long instead of facing heavy early bills.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario 2 -->
    <div class="dh-scenario-card">
      <div class="dh-scenario-header">
        <span class="dh-scenario-tag">Example 2</span>
        <strong class="dh-scenario-title">Robert, 72 — High-Cost Cancer Biologic Treatment</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="dh-scenario-intro">Robert was prescribed a specialty oral oncology drug with an initial copay of $1,200 for the first month's fill.</p>
      <table class="dh-scenario-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Input</th>
            <th>Value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Month 1 Out-of-Pocket Spend</td>
            <td>$1,200</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Month 2 Out-of-Pocket Spend</td>
            <td>$800 (Reaches $2,000 cap)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="dh-scenario-result">
        Robert reaches his $2,000 cap in <strong>February</strong>. From March 1 through December 31, he pays <span class="dh-amber-highlight">$0</span> for all covered Part D prescriptions.
      </p>
      <p class="dh-scenario-action">Robert verified with his pharmacist that his drug was on his plan's formulary (approved list) so every dollar counted toward his $2,000 cap.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="dh-scenario-disclaimer">These are representative examples based on common situations. Individual results vary based on your specific plan's formulary, deductible structure, and pharmacy network. Always verify your drug costs with <a href="https://www.medicare.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CMS (Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services)</a> or your plan administrator.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="dh-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Assuming Non-Formulary Drugs Count Toward the $2,000 Cap</h3>
    <p>Many seniors buy medications that are not on their plan's approved drug list (formulary). Out-of-pocket spending on non-formulary drugs does NOT count toward your $2,000 Part D cap.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to request a Formulary Exception from your plan administrator or ask your doctor to switch you to a covered alternative. Check official rules at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/prescription-drug-coverage-exceptions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov drug exceptions guide</a>.</p>

    <h3>Confusing Part B Infusion Drugs with Part D Outpatient Drugs</h3>
    <p>Medications administered in a doctor's office or infusion clinic (such as chemotherapy or IV antibiotics) fall under Medicare Part B (Medical Insurance), not Part D. Part B drugs are subject to 20% coinsurance and do NOT count toward the $2,000 Part D cap.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to check whether your medication is billed under Part B or Part D. Medigap Plan G covers Part B coinsurance. Learn more at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/what-medicare-covers/part-b-covered-medications" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov Part B drugs coverage</a>.</p>

    <h3>Forgetting That the $2,000 Out-of-Pocket Cap Resets Annually</h3>
    <p>The $2,000 out-of-pocket maximum is calculated per calendar year. On January 1 of each year, your out-of-pocket accumulator resets to $0 regardless of when you hit the cap the previous year.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to plan your prescription refills and consider enrolling in the Medicare Prescription Payment Plan (M3P) at the start of each new plan year at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/prescription-payment-plan" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov M3P portal</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 7 — OFFICIAL SOURCES TABLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="dh-section" aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
